<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kolyma</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kolyma Krai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kolyma is a historical region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-far-east">Russian Far East</a> covering the basin of the Kolyma River and the northern shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-okhotsk">Sea of Okhotsk</a>, with a subarctic climate of permafrost and tundra and rich gold deposits. Its indigenous peoples include the Evens, Koryaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakuts">Yakuts</a>, Chukchis and Yukaghirs. From 1932 the trust Dalstroy built Magadan and some 80 camps with forced labour, many of the prisoners swept up in the campaigns against the kulaks, and they mined gold and built the Kolyma Highway, the Road of Bones. Solzhenitsyn called Kolyma the pole of cold and cruelty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. Estimates of the dead up to the mid-1950s range from 250,000 to over a million.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolyma.jpg" alt="Kolyma" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Oxonhutch at English Wikipedia. · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Butugycheg_mine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
